              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a sewer, then the Latin for a water tower."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: porticus, porticūs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "porticus, porticūs — \"a colonnade, a covered walk, a portico\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aestāte cīvēs in porticū ambulant, quod ibi umbra est. In summer the citizens walk in the colonnade, because there is shade there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fourth declension, feminine: porticus, porticūs f. English portico and porch come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "porticus is built on porta 'gate, passage' (EtymDB): a covered passage. English portico comes from it through Italian, and porch through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
