          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aedēs, aedis — \"a temple; (plural) a house\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In forō aedēs magna et antīqua est. In the forum there is a great old temple."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, feminine: aedēs or aedis, aedis f. In the singular a temple, a house of a god; in the plural a house of people, with all its rooms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aedēs is usually traced to PIE h₂eydʰ- 'to burn', since Lewis & Short give 'hearth' as an early sense; aestās 'summer' is from the same root. English edifice comes from aedificium, built on it."
            }
          ]
        },
