              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pōmērium, pōmēriī — \"the sacred boundary (of a city)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Intrā pōmērium mīlitēs arma nōn gerunt. Inside the sacred boundary soldiers do not carry weapons."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, neuter: pōmērium, pōmēriī n., also pōmoerium. The holy line round a Roman town, marked with stones."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pōmērium is usually explained, as by ancient writers, as post 'behind' and moerus, an old form of mūrus 'wall' (Lewis & Short): the strip of land along the wall. EtymDB lists it without a parent."
            }
          ]
        },
